WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.703
This is a replay of the current saved response. It separates measured

2
00:00:05.703 --> 00:00:11.405
milk and infant exposure from observed infant outcomes, with direct source links.

3
00:00:11.405 --> 00:00:17.108
The full answer also identifies missing and truncated sections, the selected manufacturer

4
00:00:17.108 --> 00:00:21.860
label, and uncertainty about study counts. LactMed covers lactation only.

5
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.682
The little white pill question stops for identity confirmation. Appearance is not

6
00:00:28.682 --> 00:00:34.003
enough to identify a medicine. This trace has zero downstream model calls

7
00:00:34.003 --> 00:00:35.777
and zero evidence calls.

8
00:00:37.277 --> 00:00:43.189
The postpartum crisis example stops the medication workflow and returns fixed support

9
00:00:43.189 --> 00:00:49.102
referrals. It performs no diagnosis or severity assessment. Research and participation are

10
00:00:49.102 --> 00:00:53.043
suppressed, and no model generates a clinical answer.

11
00:00:54.543 --> 00:01:00.438
Daridorexant is the pre-tested gap example. The count-only tool reports source matches,

12
00:01:00.438 --> 00:01:06.333
with LactMed marked not applicable to pregnancy. These counts are different units,

13
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:12.228
not a measure of study quality. The selected label reports limited human

14
00:01:12.228 --> 00:01:18.123
pregnancy data. Empty evidence rows remain visible. The registry appears separately with

15
00:01:18.123 --> 00:01:23.527
public contacts, unranked and not recommended. Missing data never means safe.

16
00:01:25.027 --> 00:01:30.396
The synthetic no-match lookup returns machine-readable absence fields. No evidence retrieved is

17
00:01:30.396 --> 00:01:35.764
not evidence of safety. The model should preserve that distinction rather than

18
00:01:35.764 --> 00:01:38.449
turning a missing result into reassurance.

19
00:01:39.949 --> 00:01:45.719
The brief returns questions to ask a clinician about the exact product,

20
00:01:45.719 --> 00:01:51.490
the relevant population, exposure versus outcomes, missing evidence and follow-up. These questions

21
00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:57.260
are authored by the prototype. They are preparation, not a medication plan.

22
00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:03.950
Two safety risks need special emphasis. No reported effects can sound safe

23
00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:09.141
even when evidence is thin. Adverse findings can also prompt someone to

24
00:02:09.141 --> 00:02:14.331
stop a needed medicine. The payload preserves source context and includes a

25
00:02:14.331 --> 00:02:19.521
medication-change caution, and two new cases test framing. These are partial controls.

26
00:02:19.521 --> 00:02:24.712
A clinician must review real answers for reassurance and alarm that code

27
00:02:24.712 --> 00:02:26.009
checks cannot judge.

28
00:02:27.509 --> 00:02:33.348
The current source passes four hundred forty-five unit tests and exposes thirty

29
00:02:33.348 --> 00:02:39.186
tools. The evaluation compares seven paired questions on the same model, with

30
00:02:39.186 --> 00:02:45.024
retrieval tools only in the MCP arm. Automated grades are diagnostic. Routing

31
00:02:45.024 --> 00:02:50.862
and extraction remain heuristic, retrieval is bounded, and MPRINT uses snapshots. There

32
00:02:50.862 --> 00:02:56.701
is no clinician review, clinical certification or production readiness claim. New Spanish

33
00:02:56.701 --> 00:03:00.593
safety strings await human review. Nothing was deployed.

